rnnoise: add the os_support.h its ARM and scalar paths include and the tarball omits

RNNoise 0.2 cannot be compiled for arm64 as released. `src/vec.h` selects an
implementation by preprocessor:

    #if defined(__AVX__) || defined(__SSE2__)
    #include "vec_avx.h"
    #elif (defined(__ARM_NEON__) || defined(__ARM_NEON)) && !defined(DISABLE_NEON)
    #include "vec_neon.h"
    #else
    #include "os_support.h"

and both of the branches that are not `vec_avx.h` need `os_support.h` - the NEON one at
`vec_neon.h:35`, the scalar one at `vec.h:44`. That file is not in the release tarball. It
is an **Opus** header, and RNNoise shares this vectorisation code with Opus and LPCNet,
where it resolves because the file sits in the same tree.

So the NDK build fails on the first file that pulls in `vec.h`:

    src/vec_neon.h:35:10: fatal error: 'os_support.h' file not found
    make[3]: *** [src/nnet.lo] Error 1

and nothing warns beforehand, because the x86_64 host build takes the `vec_avx.h` branch
and never looks for the file. A denoiser that builds on a developer's Mac and not for the
handset is exactly the shape of defect this is.

## What is added, and what deliberately is not

One header, with three macros, copied verbatim from Opus's `celt/os_support.h`:
`OPUS_COPY`, `OPUS_MOVE`, `OPUS_CLEAR`. That is the entire surface rnnoise uses - `grep`
finds `OPUS_CLEAR` at `vec.h:53`, `:87`, `:127` and `vec_neon.h:305`, and the other two
nowhere, so they are here only because a header that defines one third of a familiar triple
is a trap for the next reader.

Opus's own `os_support.h` also wraps `malloc`/`free`/`realloc` behind
`opus_alloc`/`opus_free` and carries a stack-allocation helper. None of that is reproduced.
rnnoise calls none of it, and copying an allocator this tree does not use would be three
more definitions that can silently drift away from the Opus they were taken from.

The `0*((dst)-(src))` term in COPY and MOVE is kept exactly as Opus writes it. It is not
decoration: pointer subtraction is only valid between pointers of the same type, so the term
is a compile-time type check that turns a copy between mismatched types into a compile error
rather than a copy of the wrong number of bytes.

## Why a file and not an include path

Pointing rnnoise's `CFLAGS` at `third_party/opus/celt` would also work, and would make the
rnnoise build depend on the internal layout of a different vendored tree - so that an Opus
bump could break the denoiser, with a missing-header error naming neither. One 68-line
header inside the tree that needs it has no such edge.

`Makefile.am`'s `noinst_HEADERS` is deliberately NOT extended. It affects `make dist` and
nothing the build does, and touching `Makefile.am` would make automake want to regenerate
`Makefile.in` with whatever autotools version happens to be installed - which is precisely
the "the release tarball ships a pre-generated configure" property that made this dependency
vendorable in the first place.

Upstream: worth sending. This is a packaging bug in the 0.2 release rather than a local
preference, and the fix upstream would want is probably to ship the file rather than to add
this one - either way the report is the useful part.
diff --git a/third_party/rnnoise/src/os_support.h b/third_party/rnnoise/src/os_support.h
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..0aca35c5
--- /dev/null
+++ b/third_party/rnnoise/src/os_support.h
@@ -0,0 +1,68 @@
